---
# FILM SHARE CARDS: the worker renders each film's Open Graph card (and caches its poster and the
# card's badge-less base) into a per-country directory on k3s-worker-1 that Caddy serves at
# /share-cards/<cc>/. Cards, posters and bases share ONE budget (KINOWO_SHARE_CARD_BUDGET_MB, 1 GiB by default) that the worker's janitor
# enforces by evicting the oldest files nothing references. These rules watch the two ways that
# can go wrong -- the directory filling towards the budget, and the part the janitor may NOT delete
# outgrowing it -- the poster upstreams the cards are drawn from, and the pipeline itself: cards
# missing for the films on screen, the gauge that says so gone, and renders failing outright.
#
# AGGREGATION. The directory gauges are read from the DIRECTORY, so every replica of a country
# publishes the same number for the same files: `max by (country, kind)` first (never `sum`, which
# would count one directory once per replica), then summed over `kind` for the one budget. The
# counters are per process and are summed.
#
# RESTART-GAP-SAFE: every worker gauge is read through `last_over_time(...[10m])` and every counter
# through `increase(...[30m])`, so the ~3 minutes a pod change leaves the series absent does not
# reset a `for:` (test_alert_rule_coverage.py).
#
# The alert NAMES all start `ShareCard` so the email + Telegram route for this pipeline matches
# them by prefix; the node filesystem holding /var/lib/kinowo is watched by filesystem-capacity.rules.
groups:
  - name: share-cards
    rules:
      # 85% OF THE BUDGET, CARDS, POSTERS AND BASES TOGETHER, FOR HALF AN HOUR. Below the budget the
      # janitor is doing its job; sitting near it means the unreferenced headroom is nearly gone,
      # which is the step before `ShareCardsOverBudget`. The `kind` label names whichever of the
      # three (card, poster, base) holds the most bytes, so the annotation says where the space went.
      - alert: ShareCardsNearBudget
        expr: >-
          (
            (
              sum by (country) (max by (country, kind) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_bytes[10m])))
              / max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_budget_bytes[10m]))
            ) > 0.85
          )
          * on (country) group_left (kind)
          (0 * topk by (country) (1, max by (country, kind) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_bytes[10m]))) + 1)
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s share cards and posters use {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of their disk budget"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_share_cards_bytes` summed over cards, cached posters and card bases is above 85% of
            `kinowo_worker_share_cards_budget_bytes` for this country; most of it is {{ $labels.kind }}s.
            The janitor evicts unreferenced files oldest-first every ten minutes, so this staying
            high means most of the directory is CURRENT (the card, base and poster of every film web_movies
            points a card at) -- compare `kinowo_worker_share_cards_current_bytes`. Raise
            KINOWO_SHARE_CARD_BUDGET_MB if the corpus grew; if current bytes did not grow, look for a
            flood of re-renders in `kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total` by reason.

      # THE PART NO PRUNER MAY DELETE IS BIGGER THAN THE BUDGET. Critical: the janitor can free
      # nothing more, so the directory grows with the corpus until the node's disk does.
      - alert: ShareCardsOverBudget
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (max by (country, kind) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_current_bytes[10m])))
            > max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_budget_bytes[10m]))
          )
          * on (country) group_left (kind)
          (0 * topk by (country) (1, max by (country, kind) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_current_bytes[10m]))) + 1)
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s current share cards and posters alone exceed their disk budget"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_share_cards_current_bytes` -- the card, base and poster of every film web_movies points
            a card at, mostly {{ $labels.kind }}s -- is {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B,
            over the country's budget. The janitor never deletes current files, so nothing will
            bring this down on its own: raise KINOWO_SHARE_CARD_BUDGET_MB (and check the node's
            disk, FilesystemSpaceLow on k3s-worker-1), or find why the corpus holds more films on
            screen than before.

      # POSTER UPSTREAMS FAILING. A cache miss fetches and decodes a film's poster, walking its
      # candidates (the primary, then the cinema fallbacks); when most FILMS end with none, the new
      # cards are drawn without a poster. Counted per film (`poster_load`), not per URL
      # (`poster_fetch`): a primary that always fails -- a Multikino origin refusing the datacentre --
      # beside a fallback that works is a healthy film, and per URL it read as a 50% failure. At
      # least ten films in the window, so two failures out of three at 4am is not a page. FIRST
      # loads only: every worker boot re-tries every card drawn without a poster (~40 in pl, near
      # all failing again by nature), and on a day of deploys those bursts alone fired it
      # (2026-09-24, pl, 40 of 46 films failed per boot, all retries).
      - alert: ShareCardPosterFetchFailing
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_poster_load_total{attempt="first",result="failed"}[30m]))
            / sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_poster_load_total{attempt="first"}[30m]))
          ) > 0.5
          and on (country) sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_poster_load_total{attempt="first"}[30m])) >= 10
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s share-card posters are failing for {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of films"
          description: >-
            For more than half of the films whose poster was first fetched over the last half hour, not one
            poster URL worked -- every candidate (all its renditions, e.g. TMDB w780 then the
            original) unreachable, over the 50 MB download cap, or undecodable. Those films get a card
            WITHOUT a poster meanwhile, re-tried daily and at each worker boot. `kinowo_worker_share_cards_poster_fetch_total`
            counts the URLs, with a `reason` label saying why (http_4xx, http_5xx, timeout, too_large,
            decode_error, vips_cap, progressive_estimate, ...), and the worker logs `share card: poster
            for <film> from <host> failed: <reason>` -- check whether one host or one reason
            dominates; `kinowo_worker_http_total` does not see these fetches.

      # THE PIPELINE STALLED: FILMS ON SCREEN WITHOUT THEIR CARD. The coverage gauge is the share of
      # films with screenings whose card for their current inputs is on disk; steady state is
      # 99.8-100% (measured 2026-09-24, every country). A FRESH BACKFILL starts it from zero -- a
      # template bump re-renders every card, and the first deploy did (2026-09-24 09:50-12:10Z:
      # es in 15 minutes, de and uk in 1h30, us in 2h10, pl in 2h20). So the hold is SIX hours,
      # over twice the slowest backfill seen, and the threshold 95%, well under the daily churn of
      # new films; a pipeline that stopped rendering drifts below it as new films arrive.
      - alert: ShareCardCoverageLow
        expr: >-
          max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_coverage_ratio[10m])) < 0.95
        for: 6h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} has share cards for only {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its films on screen"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_share_cards_coverage_ratio` -- the share of films with screenings whose
            card for their CURRENT inputs exists on disk -- has been under 95% for six hours. A
            fresh backfill (a new template, a first deploy) refills from zero in under three hours
            (2026-09-24: pl, the slowest, in 2h20m), so the backfill is not keeping up or not
            running. Read `kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total` by outcome: quiet means no
            renders are being queued (is the ShareCardBackfill task running?); busy with `failed`
            means ShareCardRendersFailing is the alert to read. An uncovered film's page names the
            card drawn for its previous inputs, or the city's static card if it never had one
            (web ShareCardUrl), and link previews keep whichever they fetch.

      # THE GAUGE ABOVE IS GONE WHILE THE WORKER IS UP. Per country, not `absent()` over the whole
      # metric: one country's backfill not running must not hide behind the others' series. A
      # worker that is down is WorkerDown's (its `up` is 0, so it drops out here). One hour, as the
      # other *MetricsAbsent rules: longer than a fleet rollout; the gauge is published within a
      # couple of minutes of a boot (2026-09-24's restarts left 5-6 minute gaps).
      - alert: ShareCardCoverageAbsent
        expr: >-
          max by (country) (last_over_time(up{job="kinowo-worker"}[10m]) == 1)
          unless on (country)
          max by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_share_cards_coverage_ratio[10m]))
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s worker is up but publishes no share-card coverage"
          description: >-
            The worker answers its scrape (`up`) but has published no
            `kinowo_worker_share_cards_coverage_ratio` for this country for an hour, so
            ShareCardCoverageLow cannot fire and a stalled card pipeline goes unseen. The
            ShareCardBackfill tick publishes it every minute while the country has films on
            screen; its absence means the tick is not running, or the gauge was unwired from the
            worker's metrics. A worker that is DOWN is WorkerDown's to report, not this one's.

      # RENDERS FAILING OUTRIGHT. `failed` is a card that could not be drawn even without its
      # poster -- a template, font or libvips fault, not an upstream (a dead poster is
      # `rendered_no_poster`, above). NOT ZERO DURING A BACKFILL: pl's first one (2026-09-24
      # 11:10-11:45Z) failed 93 `new_film` renders, peaking at 12% of the half hour's; every other
      # country, none. So a quarter, twice that peak, of at least ten renders in the half hour, held
      # for half an hour: a backfill's stragglers are not a page, a broken template failing every
      # card is.
      - alert: ShareCardRendersFailing
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total{outcome="failed"}[30m]))
            / sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total[30m]))
          ) > 0.25
          and on (country) sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total[30m])) >= 10
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s share-card renders are failing: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of the last half hour's"
          description: >-
            More than a quarter of the last half hour's `kinowo_worker_share_cards_render_total`
            ended `failed`: the card could not be drawn at all, not even without its poster (a
            poster-less card is `rendered_no_poster`, which ShareCardPosterFetchFailing watches).
            Each failed task is retried up to three times and then given up, so those films keep
            the city fallback card and coverage falls. The `reason` label names the input change
            that queued the failing renders, and the worker logs `share card: <film> could not be
            drawn: <exception>`. After a deploy, suspect the template, the fonts or libvips.
